out and to play you know  against uh tony lung was also like a  dream come true so  yeah i i was super excited going to  training  what does it mean to you to be a part of  this project what do you hope it'll  represent for audiences and what kind of  excitement are you hearing from your own  family and friends  my own family um  they actually  they like a lot of asian parents like  they they're like we don't know if it's  good till we see it we don't know if  you're good till we actually see it so  they haven't seen the film so they're  kind of like you know a little bit like  sitting back and waiting for it um but  when they heard you know i had to  explain to them  how big you know a marvel film is and  how important it is to  uh to have of you know a asian  lead film in the marvel universe and you  know for the world to see  and then my parents you know finally  understood how big of a film this is and  and then my dad really got excited over  my little figures um so he asked me to  like buy like dozens of them and i had  to sign all of them and my dad took all  of them and he's he's like literally  right now like distributing among our  relatives friends and everybody so i  guess in that sense like i think they're  really excited about it but um they  haven't they usually never praised me  for anything i've done  right or but like good um so  uh i did so i'm not expecting  compliments coming from their way but  yeah you know if my parents say no not  bad and that's pretty good then i i  think it's the biggest um compliment in  the world  that's awesome  well what what did you most enjoy about  the fighting style that you got to do in  the training that you did for this and  what was the biggest challenge in  how you had to approach that  um from the physical aspect of things i  think  i really enjoyed tai chi i fell in love  with tai chi which inspired my  character's fighting style um because  tai chi is  not super masculine it's not super like  you know pushing and strong but it's has  so much power in its um  movements and it's so smooth and it's  so subtle  it takes years of training to even like  understand you know how to balance every  move it's constantly in motion  it's beautiful to watch i got obsessed  with it and i was just like on youtube  all the time watching different tai chi  competitions and different  demonstrations um  but yeah and i really loved  being  flying around in air  on a wire  and that was like my first time in a  lollipop  you know twirling in the air 360  learning how to flip and doing all that  but also i had i was  lucky enough to work with the stunt team  and my stunt double who took on some of  the most dangerous movements that i  could do myself so um shout out to a  snow snow fan that's her name  um  just to like help me to complete all of  my uh movements  but yeah it was definitely a great  learning opportunity for me to fall in  love with tai chi  but i and i think you know with the  magic of marvel  movie magic that elevated our movements  and the scenes um yeah it's just  visually stunning to  watch obviously some of these fight  scenes are so graceful and beautiful do  they feel like that when you're shooting  them do you have mishaps or awkward  moments happening  oh yeah all the time uh  it it feels and i i was imagining  something similar to this but you know i  i could never imagine what it looked  like fully when you know after they've  done all the editing and everything but  um yeah there were times where i just  like  wasn't sure like if i was doing it right  because the tai chi movement is so  how do i describe it it's so precise but  it also requires you to be so free in  your form that you don't have to think  about it you know you can't look like  you're thinking about it has become so  natural you know so finding that balance  and to like you know  i'm an actor after all i'm not a you  know i'm not a martial artist so like to  really like focus on the acting part of  it as well it was a lot to to juggle but  um  yeah i think with multiple takes and you  know trial and error and um having the  best support and destin really letting  us to freely try different things and  and all of that i'm glad it came out  really beautifully  you started your career as a film and tv  actor in asia before  going to juilliard and now you're  working in hollywood productions so what  what most surprised you about being on a  set like this  just how big everything is on this set i  mean i've been  to quite big sets but you know just how  many people  are working on set and behind the scenes  um i think the fans like you you just  don't know like there's literally  hundreds of people on set um it's like  an army of people trying to make you  know such an you know even scenes with  you know just you see two actors on  screen  there's just so many moving parts um  that was happening behind the scenes um  and i was just in awe of you know  the producers who can put you know such  great team together  um and yeah and then i it was kind of my  first time stepping back onto a film set  after five years of  not working in front of the screen i was  in school doing theater  um just stepping back it feels  it felt very familiar at the same time  really fresh and new  um it was incredible feeling  there's beautiful themes of family in  this what did you most love about that  aspect of the story  um i'm incredibly close to my family and  i  think family is such an important um  um  thing in in asian culture especially um  so i think this film really like spoke  to me personally but i think  hopefully the audience will resonate  with that as well not only you know  asian audiences but also  um audiences from all over the world  that they can everyone anyone can relate  to  um you know the love the unconditional  love that you share  among a family you know between family  members and um and the fact that they  know no family is perfect  we all have our differences  misunderstandings up and downs and i i  think i hope people can see that you  know  we are who we are because of our family  because of our ancestry  and and respect that and um and i think  given what's happening to especially our  elders um older asian  um  people in america  um  i feel like i hope the world understand  how much we value our  our older generations our parents our  grandparents who  who worked so hard and sacrificed so  much for us  the new generation  that i hope people respect them more and  give them peace and you know the respect  that they deserve  well thank you so much for talking about  this beautiful work in the film thank  you  thank you
 

